155: Telegram Bot MCP Server
156: MCP (Model Context Protocol) server for Telegram Bot API. Send messages, manage chats, handle webhooks, and more through 27 tools.
157: Works with Claude Desktop, Cursor, VS Code, and any MCP client.
158: Quick Start
159: Claude Desktop
160: Add to claude_desktop_config.json:
161: {
162: "mcpServers": {
163: "telegram": {
164: "command": "npx",
165: "args": ["-y", "@node2flow/telegram-bot-mcp"],
166: "env": {
167: "TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN": "your-bot-token"
168: }
169: }
170: }
171: }
172: Cursor / VS Code
173: Add to MCP settings:
174: {
175: "mcpServers": {
176: "telegram": {
177: "command": "npx",
178: "args": ["-y", "@node2flow/telegram-bot-mcp"],
179: "env": {
180: "TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN": "your-bot-token"
181: }
182: }
183: }
184: }
185: HTTP Mode (Streamable HTTP)
186: For remote deployment or shared access:
187: TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=your_token npx @node2flow/telegram-bot-mcp --http
188: Server starts on port 3000 (configurable via PORT env var). MCP endpoint: http://localhost:3000/mcp
189: Configuration
190: Environment Variable
191: Required
192: Description
193: TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN
194: Yes
195: Telegram Bot token from @BotFather
196: PORT
197: No
198: Port for HTTP server (default: 3000, only used with --http)

4: # Webhooks
5: > Get notified when a routine run finishes, then verify and handle the delivery.
6: Webhooks let Clay notify your service when a routine run finishes, so you can react without polling. Register a webhook, pass its id when you start a run, and handle the signed `POST` Clay sends once results are ready.
7: ## Register a webhook
8: Create a webhook with the CLI — run `clay webhooks --help` for the full command surface (create, list, test, delete):
9: ```bash theme={"theme":{"light":"github-light","dark":"github-dark"}}
10: clay webhooks create https://example.com/hooks/clay
11: ```
12: `create` returns the signing secret exactly once — store it immediately, it can't be retrieved later:
13: ```json theme={"theme":{"light":"github-light","dark":"github-dark"}}
14: {
15: "id": "wh_abc123",
16: "url": "https://example.com/hooks/clay",
17: "createdAt": "2026-06-16T17:50:00.000Z",
18: "signingSecret": "whsec_..."
19: }
20: ```
21: ## Trigger a delivery
22: Pass the webhook id as `webhook_id` when you start a routine run (see [Routines](https://developers.clay.com/routines/api.md)). Clay sends a signed `POST` to the webhook URL when the run is ready to read.
23: ## Delivery payload
24: ```json theme={"theme":{"light":"github-light","dark":"github-dark"}}
25: {
26: "webhookId": "wh_abc123",
27: "createdAt": "2026-06-16T17:50:00.000Z",
28: "data": {
29: "routine_run_id": "run_abc123"
30: }
31: }
32: ```
33: For test events, `data` is `{}`. The `routine_run_id` can identify an inline or a bulk run. If one webhook handles both, store the run mode when you start the run, or use `clay routines runs get`, which fetches results for either mode.
34: ## Verify and fetch results
35: Clay signs the exact request body with your webhook signing secret and sends it in the `X-Clay-Signature` header. Verify the signature before trusting a delivery, then fetch the run's results.
36: ```javascript theme={"theme":{"light":"github-light","dark":"github-dark"}}
37: import crypto from 'node:crypto';
38: import express from 'express';
39: const app = express();
40: function sign(body) {
41: return `sha256=${crypto
42: .createHmac('sha256', process.env.CLAY_WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET)
43: .update(body, 'utf8')
44: .digest('hex')}`;
45: }
46: function signaturesMatch(actual, expected) {
47: const actualBuffer = Buffer.from(actual);
48: const expectedBuffer = Buffer.from(expected);
49: return actualBuffer.length === expectedBuffer.length && crypto.timingSafeEqual(actualBuffer, expectedBuffer);
50: }
51: async function fetchRunResults(routineRunId) {
52: // For batch routine runs, use `/public/v0/routines/run-batch/${routineRunId}/results`.
53: const result = await fetch(`https://api.clay.com/public/v0/routines/run/${routineRunId}/results`, {
54: headers: { 'clay-api-key': process.env.CLAY_PUBLIC_API_KEY },
55: });
56: return result.json();
57: }
58: app.post('/hooks/clay', express.raw({ type: 'application/json' }), async (req, res) => {

